Theorem 1.66. For any nonempty A, the following are equivalent:

   1. A is countable;
   2. there exists a surjection f : N → A;
   3. there exists an injection g : A → N.

Definition 2.25. Dendrogram (finite case). Let X be finite. A dendrogram
on X is an order preserving map θ : [0, ∞) → P art(X) on X such that:
  1. θ(0) is the discrete partition of X;
  2. there exists T > 0 such that θ(T ) is the trivial partition;

  3. for any t ≥ 0, there exists ϵ > 0 such that θ(t) = θ(t + ϵ).

Example 3.22. S = {{0, 1}, {0, 2}} is a subbasis on X = {0, 1, 2}. It is clear
that S is not a basis. The topology generated by S is τS = {∅, {0}, {0, 1}, {0, 2}, X}
with a basis {{0}, {0, 1}, {0, 2}}.

Theorem 3.42. If B is a basis for τX and B ′ is a basis for τY , then

                         B × B ′ = {U × V : U ∈ B, V ∈ B ′ }

is a basis for the product topology on X × Y .

Theorem 3.45. The collection

                    S = {π1−1 (U ) : U ∈ τX } ∪ {π2−1 (V ) : V ∈ τY }

is a subbasis for the product topology on X × Y .

Theorem 5.16. For a second countable space X,
   • (Lindelöf property) every open cover of X has a countable subcover;
   • X is separable.

Theorem 6.1 (Intermediate Value Theorem). Let f : [0, 1] → R be a continuous
function. WLOG, we assume that f (0) < f (1). Then, for any r ∈ [f (0), f (1)],
there exists c ∈ (0, 1) such that f (c) = r.

Lemma 6.64. Let X be regular. Then, TFAE: Every open covering U of X
has a refinement that is:
  1. a locally finite open cover;
  2. A countably locally finite open cover, i.e., it can be written as a countable
     union of locally finite collections;
  3. a locally finite cover;
  4. a locally finite closed cover.

